Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["8. Four Fermions And One Photon", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"07c04d69-a340-4d6a-ba03-a71e9a0b5856"],

Cell["\<\
The other two nonzero five-point sectors are e, E, e, E, A.+ and e, E, e, E, \
A.-. Start with the eight actual diagrams. In each radiating fermion line, \
Dirac reduction and Schouten expose a numerator multiple of that diagram's \
electron on-shell condition. Select the representative without that multiple \
while keeping both original propagator denominators off shell. Combining the \
two attachments then cancels the reference spinor exactly. Fermion exchange \
signs combine the four radiating currents. The negative-photon result follows \
by exchanging angle and square spinors.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"16b836bf-5d5f-4bce-84ca-30e275cd070b"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
qedFourFermionDiagrams = CreateDiagrams[
  {\"e\", \"E\", \"e\", \"E\", \"A.+\"}, qedDiagramModel];
qedFourFermionDerivation = q5FourFermionPhotonRoute[qedFourFermionDiagrams];
qedFourFermionPositiveAmplitude = qedFourFermionDerivation[\"Final\"];
(qedFourFermionPositiveAmplitude // nice) /. nice[parameter_Symbol] :> \
parameter
\
\>", "Input",
 CellLabel->
  "In[128]:=",ExpressionUUID->"c6001be3-28da-4b4e-8435-1a8fc01e2a9f"],

